# What may cross the FFI boundary, asked of a declaration — and where a
# boundary type may STAND, which is a different question (design.md
# §4.19; panels 036, 038, 060, 062).
# Port of crates/heroes/src/types/{ffi_decls,ffi_position}.rs — two Rust
# files, one Heroes module: position is asked only by the signature
# check, and the two questions read together.
#
# All four declaration checks refuse in the LOUD direction (CLAUDE.md
# §11): a container or a record with no header behind it has no C
# counterpart, so leaving it to clang costs an internal error naming
# generated C at exit 2 — the compiler blaming itself for a mistake in a
# .hero file, which is the failure §4.19's own guarantee exists to
# prevent.
#
# The position file's own history rides along: the argument-refusal list
# was two entries for about a minute, and the second (`str`) was refuted
# BY THIS REPOSITORY on its first run — hero_os.h takes a HeroStr by
# value. A premise about which headers exist cannot see a header written
# for this language; `()` survives because its premise is about C's
# GRAMMAR, and a grammar does not acquire new headers.

use token
use ast
use source
use parse
use resolve
use check/table
use check/state
use check/lower
use ffi_errors

## The vocabulary — can a C header spell this at all?

# What a C header can spell: the scalars, §4.19's two opaque types, (),
# a function pointer — since panel 111 R4 — and, since panel 060, a
# record THE HEADER ITSELF declares. The narrowing asks the
# declaration, never where the type was mentioned: "this record carries
# a header" is a fact about the value in hand.
#
# A function type answers YES here and is still refused at two of its
# three positions, which is the whole reason this question and
# arguments_may_stand's are asked separately. C spells a function
# pointer everywhere it spells a type, so a vocabulary rule cannot be
# the one that says where a callback may stand.
#
# **AND IT ANSWERS YES ONLY IF WHAT IT CARRIES CAN CROSS — the arm below
# recurses, and it did not until defect 014** (panel 113 R2, 2026-09-06).
# `.function_ty => return true` judged the callback and never what the
# callback takes and returns, so a `[T]` or a `{K: V}` crossed inside a
# callback signature, in both directions, at **exit 0 with an empty
# stderr**. The bar was one forward declaration:
#
#     struct HeroArrayHeader;
#     void hold(struct HeroArrayHeader *(*mk)(void),
#               void (*run)(struct HeroArrayHeader *));
#
# `spec:224` had already refused it — *"a callback is a parameter, never
# a result; ITS PARAMETERS FOLLOW THE SAME RULE"* — and the same rule is
# this function, which refuses `.array` one line down. So this was the
# compiler disagreeing with the specification (CLAUDE.md §12), not a
# design question, and it cost no spec token to settle.
#
# WHY IT MATTERED MORE THAN IT LOOKS. That was the only route by which a
# refcounted Heroes value could reach a thread the program did not start.
# Panel 113 was convened on `cow.c`'s `if (refcount == 1)` and two seats
# measured, independently and with a live positive control, that the
# corruption does not arrive through there: a callback parameter comes in
# BORROWED and the emitted body increfs before it can store, so `unshare`
# never sees the window. The door was here.
#
# The recursion terminates for `poisoned`'s reason at
# `selfhost/check/table.hero:278-285`, which walks the same interned
# table the same way: a type id is an index into a table built before
# this pass runs, so the walk is over a finite acyclic structure and not
# over the program.
function crosses_the_boundary(c: state.Checker, decls: [ast.Decl], ty: i64) -> bool
    match table.get(c.out.types, ty)
        .named n =>
            match decls[n.decl].kind
                .record_decl rec                                             => return !rec.header.is_err()
                .constant_decl | .function_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => return false
        .int_ty | .float_ty | .bool | .str | .ptr | .cstr | .unit | .error => return true
        .function_ty f =>
            for p in table.params_of(c.out.types, f.params)
                if !crosses_the_boundary(c, decls, p)
                    return false

            return crosses_the_boundary(c, decls, f.result)
        .array | .fixed | .map | .fallible => return false
        .case_ty | .failure | .generic     => return false

    return false

# Every type in an extern's signature must be one a C header can declare.
# Where each may STAND is the position check's question, asked after this
# one so a type refused at both gets one message.
function ffi_signature(@c: state.Checker, a: ast.Ast, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], f: ast.FunctionDecl, result: i64, declared_name: token.Span)
    if !crosses_the_boundary(c, decls, result)
        refused(@c, s, decls, ty: result, what: "an `extern`'s result", span: a.types[f.result].span)

    for param in f.params
        written = state.written_type(c.out, param.ty)

        if written.is_err()
            continue

        if !crosses_the_boundary(c, decls, written.must())
            refused(@c, s, decls, ty: written.must(), what: "an `extern`'s parameter", span: a.types[param.ty].span)

    result_may_stand(@c, a, s, decls, f: f, result: result, declared_name: declared_name)
    arguments_may_stand(@c, a, s, decls, f: f, declared_name: declared_name)

# One refusal, and it picks the message by WHERE the refused type is.
# A callback that is itself fine and carries something that is not gets
# the level named; everything else gets the vocabulary message. Splitting
# it here rather than at the two call sites is what keeps the result and
# the parameter saying the same thing about the same type.
function refused(@c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], ty: i64, what: str, span: token.Span)
    carried = carried_refusal(c, decls, ty)

    if carried >= 0
        state.push_diagnostic(
            @c,
            ffi_errors.ffi_callback_carries(callback: state.show(c, s, decls, ty), carried: state.show(c, s, decls, carried), what: what, span: span)
        )
        return
    state.push_diagnostic(
        @c,
        ffi_errors.ffi_type(name: state.show(c, s, decls, ty), what: what, span: span)
    )

# The first type INSIDE a callback that cannot cross, or -1 when the
# refusal is not of that shape. Parameters before the result, in the
# order written, so the message names the leftmost thing the reader has
# to change — which is where their eye already is.
function carried_refusal(c: state.Checker, decls: [ast.Decl], ty: i64) -> i64
    match table.get(c.out.types, ty)
        .function_ty f =>
            for p in table.params_of(c.out.types, f.params)
                if !crosses_the_boundary(c, decls, p)
                    return p

            if !crosses_the_boundary(c, decls, f.result)
                return f.result

            return -1
        .named _ | .int_ty | .float_ty | .bool | .str | .ptr | .cstr | .unit | .error => return -1
        .array | .fixed | .map | .fallible | .case_ty | .failure | .generic           => return -1

    return -1

# The type of an extern constant — a narrower question (panel 038). Two
# of the seven boundary types cannot be a VALUE a header holds, and both
# refusals are facts about Heroes rather than about C headers. `bool` is
# deliberately NOT refused here: "no c11 header constant is _Bool" is a
# premise about the world, and the per-constant assertion asks the token
# in hand instead.
function ffi_constant(@c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], declared: i64, span: token.Span)
    if !crosses_the_boundary(c, decls, declared)
        shown = state.show(c, s, decls, declared)
        state.push_diagnostic(@c, ffi_errors.ffi_type(name: shown, what: "an `extern constant`", span: span))
        return
    why: str @ ""

    match table.get(c.out.types, declared)
        .str  => why @ "a `str` is built by this runtime, so no C header holds one: declare it `cstr` and read it with `validated`, which copies and may fail (§4.20)"
        .unit => why @ "a `constant` names a value, and `()` is a type rather than a value"
        # Panel 111 R4 opened the parameter position and nothing else,
        # and a `constant` is not a position: it is a VALUE this
        # compiler hands the program, so the address in it would be
        # C's and calling it would run a body no Heroes emitter wrote —
        # result_may_stand's case, reached through the one door that
        # does not go through a signature.

        .function_ty                                        => why @ "a function value Heroes calls must be one this compiler emitted, and this asks the header for one of C's. Declare it `ptr` if the program only hands it back to C"
        .int_ty | .float_ty | .bool | .ptr | .cstr | .error => return
        .array | .fixed | .map | .fallible | .named         => return
        .case_ty | .failure | .generic                      => return

    shown = state.show(c, s, decls, declared)
    state.push_diagnostic(@c, ffi_errors.ffi_constant_type(name: shown, why: why, span: span))

# What a group's record may hold — deliberately NARROWER than the
# signature vocabulary, and the two omissions are the whole difference
# between a signature and a struct: a str crosses as an ARGUMENT to a
# function that knows what it is, and no C header declares one as a
# member; () is a legal result and cannot be a field.
function ffi_field(@c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], ty: i64, span: token.Span)
    fine = match table.get(c.out.types, ty)
        .int_ty | .float_ty | .bool | .ptr | .cstr | .error => true
        # i32[4] — a C array member, and its element is the same question
        # again (panel 062: it is the last field form raylib needs, and
        # the element is asked recursively because Matrix projection[2]
        # is an array OF a group record).

        .fixed x => fixed_element_fine(c, decls, x.element)
        # A nested record is fine exactly when it is also the header's —
        # RenderTexture holds two Textures by value, and both are raylib's.

        .named n                                                => header_owned(decls, n.decl)
        .str | .unit | .array | .map | .fallible | .function_ty => false
        .case_ty | .failure | .generic                          => false

    if fine
        return

    # The note is chosen by the type, because the repair is (§4.17): a
    # note about str's reference count printed under an [i64] field sends
    # the reader to the wrong repair with full confidence.
    why = match table.get(c.out.types, ty)
        .str => "a `str` is this runtime's own value, with a reference count before its bytes, so no C struct holds one: declare the field `cstr` and read it with `validated`, which copies and may fail"
        .array | .map => "a C struct holds a pointer to its elements, never the elements: declare the field `ptr` and reach them through the header's own accessors"
        .fallible => "`T?` is this language's own two-word value and no header declares one: a C function reports failure in its result or an out-parameter (§4.19)"
        .unit => "`()` is a type rather than a value, so nothing can hold one — drop the field"
        .named => "a record can hold another only if the header declares that one too: move it into this `extern` group, or declare the field `ptr` if C holds a pointer to it"
        # Was "no C header can declare a field of this type" — §11's false
        # claim about the world; tiffio.h has float d_mat[3][3] (panel 081).

        .int_ty | .float_ty | .bool | .ptr | .cstr | .error => "this group has no spelling for that: a field is a number, `bool`, `ptr`, `cstr`, another record of the group, or a fixed array of one"
        .fixed | .function_ty | .case_ty | .failure | .generic => "this group has no spelling for that: a field is a number, `bool`, `ptr`, `cstr`, another record of the group, or a fixed array of one"

    shown = state.show(c, s, decls, ty)
    state.push_diagnostic(@c, ffi_errors.ffi_field_type(name: shown, why: why, span: span))

function fixed_element_fine(c: state.Checker, decls: [ast.Decl], element: i64) -> bool
    return match table.get(c.out.types, element)
        .int_ty | .float_ty | .bool | .ptr | .cstr | .error              => true
        .named n                                                         => header_owned(decls, n.decl)
        .str | .unit | .array | .fixed | .map | .fallible | .function_ty => false
        .case_ty | .failure | .generic                                   => false

function header_owned(decls: [ast.Decl], decl: i64) -> bool
    return match decls[decl].kind
        .record_decl rec                                             => !rec.header.is_err()
        .constant_decl | .function_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => false

# A fixed array where no header owns the layout (panel 062).
function fixed_outside_group(@c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], ty: i64, span: token.Span)
    is_fixed = match table.get(c.out.types, ty)
        .fixed                                                    => true
        .int_ty | .float_ty | .bool | .str | .ptr | .cstr | .unit => false
        .array | .map | .fallible | .function_ty | .named         => false
        .case_ty | .failure | .generic | .error                   => false

    if !is_fixed
        return
    shown = state.show(c, s, decls, ty)
    state.push_diagnostic(@c, ffi_errors.fixed_outside_a_group(shown, span))

## The position — may it stand here?

# A function value C hands BACK is refused, and the reason is not about
# C's grammar: C returns function pointers, and `signal` is the header
# every reader meets first. It is about what the value would BE on this
# side. A Heroes function value is called through a Heroes call site,
# which converts the arguments and holds the result the way this
# language does — against a body no Heroes emitter wrote, and which no
# probe ever compared to the header. The parameter direction is safe for
# the mirror reason, and that mirror is the whole permission: the
# address handed OUT is one this compiler emitted, so the call C makes
# is a call into code this compiler type-checked.
function result_may_stand(@c: state.Checker, a: ast.Ast, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], f: ast.FunctionDecl, result: i64, declared_name: token.Span)
    if !table.is_function_ty(c.out.types, result)
        return
    shown = state.show(c, s, decls, result)
    state.push_diagnostic(
        @c,
        ffi_errors.ffi_result_position(function_name: source.span_text(s, declared_name), name: shown, span: a.types[f.result].span)
    )

# Refuse every parameter whose declared type cannot stand in a C argument
# list. Runs AFTER the vocabulary check, so a type that can cross at
# neither position produces one message rather than two. Two shapes are
# refused, and the test for whether a refusal belongs in the frontend at
# all is: is the fact about C's GRAMMAR, or about what the value would
# be on this side? A parameter list has no spelling for void; an `@`
# parameter of function type is result_may_stand's own case wearing
# C's other direction, since `void (**)(void)` delivers a callable into
# Heroes exactly as a returned one does. A ptr parameter is ordinary C
# only clang can judge against the header in front of it.
function arguments_may_stand(@c: state.Checker, a: ast.Ast, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], f: ast.FunctionDecl, declared_name: token.Span)
    function_name = source.span_text(s, declared_name)

    for param in f.params
        written = state.written_type(c.out, param.ty)

        if written.is_err()
            continue
        why: str @ ""

        if table.get(c.out.types, written.must()) == .unit
            why @ "`()` names no value, so there is no argument to pass: as a *result* it is C's `void`, which a parameter list has no spelling for. Drop the parameter, or declare the type the header uses"

        if param.mutable && table.is_function_ty(c.out.types, written.must())
            why @ "an `@` parameter is C's out-parameter, so this asks C to WRITE a function value here, and a function value Heroes calls must be one this compiler emitted. Declare it `@" + source.span_text(s, param.name) + ": ptr` if the program only stores what C writes, and pass it back to C to be called"

        if why == ""
            continue
        shown = state.show(c, s, decls, written.must())
        state.push_diagnostic(
            @c,
            ffi_errors.ffi_parameter_position(parameter: source.span_text(s, param.name), function_name: function_name, name: shown, why: why, span: a.types[param.ty].span)
        )

## Tests

function prepared(text: str) -> state.Checker
    p = parse.parse(text)
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text)
    out = resolve.resolve(p.tree, s, p.decls, p.uses)
    c: state.Checker @ state.new_checker(out_of: out, expr_count: p.tree.exprs.len())
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < p.tree.types.len()
        _ = lower.lower_ty(@c, p.tree, out, i)
        i @ i + 1

    return c

test "the vocabulary admits the seven and the group's own records"
    text = "extern \"r.h\"\n    record Color\n        r: u8\n    function tint(c: Color) -> Color\n\nrecord Mine\n    n: i64\n"
    p = parse.parse(text)
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text)
    out = resolve.resolve(p.tree, s, p.decls, p.uses)
    c: state.Checker @ state.new_checker(out_of: out, expr_count: 0)
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < p.tree.types.len()
        _ = lower.lower_ty(@c, p.tree, out, i)
        i @ i + 1

    color = table.intern(@c.out.types, .named(decl: 0))
    mine = table.intern(@c.out.types, .named(decl: 2))
    assert crosses_the_boundary(c, p.decls, color)
    assert !crosses_the_boundary(c, p.decls, mine)
    assert crosses_the_boundary(c, p.decls, table.str_ty(c.out.types))
    xs = table.intern(@c.out.types, .array(element: table.int_ty_id(c.out.types)))
    assert !crosses_the_boundary(c, p.decls, xs)

test "an extern constant refuses the two Heroes-only values with reasons"
    c: state.Checker @ prepared("record P\n    x: i64\n")
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: "record P\n    x: i64\n")
    d: [ast.Decl] = []
    span = token.Span(start: 0, end: 3)
    ffi_constant(@c, s, d, declared: table.str_ty(c.out.types), span: span)
    assert c.out.diagnostics.len() == 1
    assert c.out.diagnostics[0].code == "ffi_constant_type"
    ffi_constant(@c, s, d, declared: table.int_ty_id(c.out.types), span: span)
    assert c.out.diagnostics.len() == 1

test "a group field takes a fixed array of scalars, and a str is routed"
    c: state.Checker @ prepared("record P\n    x: i64\n")
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: "record P\n    x: i64\n")
    d: [ast.Decl] = []
    span = token.Span(start: 0, end: 3)
    f32_id = table.intern(@c.out.types, .float_ty(kind: .f32))
    lens = table.intern(@c.out.types, .fixed(element: f32_id, length: 4))
    ffi_field(@c, s, d, ty: lens, span: span)
    assert c.out.diagnostics.len() == 0
    ffi_field(@c, s, d, ty: table.str_ty(c.out.types), span: span)
    assert c.out.diagnostics.len() == 1
    assert c.out.diagnostics[0].code == "ffi_field_type"
    assert c.out.diagnostics[0].notes[0] == "a `str` is this runtime's own value, with a reference count before its bytes, so no C struct holds one: declare the field `cstr` and read it with `validated`, which copies and may fail"

test "a unit parameter cannot stand, though a unit result may"
    text = "extern \"m.h\"\n    function odd(n: ()) -> i32\n"
    p = parse.parse(text)
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text)
    out = resolve.resolve(p.tree, s, p.decls, p.uses)
    c: state.Checker @ state.new_checker(out_of: out, expr_count: 0)
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < p.tree.types.len()
        _ = lower.lower_ty(@c, p.tree, out, i)
        i @ i + 1

    match p.decls[0].kind
        .function_decl fd =>
            result = state.written_type(c.out, fd.value.result).must()
            ffi_signature(@c, p.tree, s, p.decls, f: fd.value, result: result, declared_name: p.decls[0].name)
        .constant_decl | .record_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => assert false

    assert c.out.diagnostics.len() == 1
    assert c.out.diagnostics[0].code == "ffi_parameter_type"
